[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"Understanding Unity Render Pipelines: Built-in, URP, and HDRP"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"Choosing the right render pipeline is one of the most critical architectural decisions you will make in Unity. Your choice impacts performance, visual fidelity, target hardware, and the tools available to your art and technical teams. Unity currently offers three distinct rendering solutions."},{"order":3,"type":"subheading","text":"1. The Built-in Render Pipeline"},{"order":4,"type":"paragraph","text":"The Built-in Render Pipeline is Unity's traditional default renderer. While mature and supported, it has largely been superseded by Scriptable Render Pipelines (SRP) for new projects. It uses a fixed-function approach with limited extensibility via custom shaders written in standard HLSL/CG."},{"order":5,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Pros: Extremely familiar, massive asset store compatibility from older projects, and lightweight out-of-the-box."},{"order":6,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Cons: Difficult to customize rendering paths, poor scalability across diverse hardware, and lacks modern optimization features like SRP Batcher."},{"order":7,"type":"subheading","text":"2. Universal Render Pipeline (URP)"},{"order":8,"type":"paragraph","text":"URP is a Scriptable Render Pipeline designed to put performance and adaptability first. It aims to make it easy to create optimized, high-fidelity graphics across a wide range of platforms, from mobile devices to high-end PCs and consoles."},{"order":9,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Optimized Performance: Utilizes a Single-Pass Forward rendering path with batching optimizations (SRP Batcher)."},{"order":10,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Shader Graph Support: Fully compatible with Unity's node-based Shader Graph for rapid visual material creation."},{"order":11,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Cross-Platform: Excellent scaling for mobile, VR, Nintendo Switch, and standard desktop titles."},{"order":12,"type":"subheading","text":"3. High Definition Render Pipeline (HDRP)"},{"order":13,"type":"paragraph","text":"HDRP is built for high-end, compute-shader-heavy hardware (PC, PlayStation, Xbox). It utilizes a Scriptable Render Pipeline focused on cutting-edge graphical fidelity, physically-based lighting, and advanced volumetric effects."},{"order":14,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Visual Quality: Supports Physically Based Rendering (PBR), Subsurface Scattering, Volumetric Clouds, and Ray Tracing."},{"order":15,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Lighting: Offers both Deferred and Forward lighting architectures with physical light units (Lux, Lumens)."},{"order":16,"type":"subheading","text":"Programmatically Accessing Render Pipelines in Code"},{"order":17,"type":"paragraph","text":"Sometimes your game logic needs to know which render pipeline is currently active to adjust settings dynamically. You can check the active pipeline asset using Unity's GraphicsSettings API."},{"order":18,"type":"code","text":"using UnityEngine;
using UnityEngine.Rendering;

public class PipelineDetector : MonoBehaviour
{
    void Start()
    {
        RenderPipelineAsset currentPipeline = GraphicsSettings.currentRenderPipeline;

        if (currentPipeline == null)
        {
            Debug.Log("Using Built-in Render Pipeline");
        }
        else if (currentPipeline.GetType().Name.Contains("Universal"))
        {
            Debug.Log("Using Universal Render Pipeline (URP)");
        }
        else if (currentPipeline.GetType().Name.Contains("HighDefinition"))
        {
            Debug.Log("Using High Definition Render Pipeline (HDRP)");
        }
    }
}"},{"order":19,"type":"subheading","text":"Conclusion and Recommendation"},{"order":20,"type":"paragraph","text":"For 90% of new projects, URP is the recommended choice due to its balance of modern features, performance, and multi-platform reach. Choose HDRP only if you are targeting high-end PC/consoles with a strong technical art team. Avoid the Built-in pipeline for new developments unless maintaining legacy codebases."}]
